messages:
emily: FYI customer on Chrome 117 sees silent gaps when using the SDK's fetch-based SSE polyfill. They get partial JSON objects (truncated) then the client reconnects and sometimes receives the rest duplicated. Trace id: 0a7c2f9b. SDK version: js@1.4.2. Repro attached below.

priya: thanks, can you paste the minimal repro and the network logs (HAR) if available? any auth token rotation?

emily: no token 401s in logs. Token rotation occurs every 10m in their app but the disconnects happen much faster (20-40s). HAR shows long silence (no bytes) for ~18s then connection close. They reported spikes in latency at the same times. HAR + console: `EventSource polyfill: stream ended`

sam: @emily can you confirm if this is using native EventSource or a fetch ReadableStream approach? different codepaths in SDK.

emily: fetch ReadableStream. Customer uses the small polyfill we document. Minimal repro snippet below (they ran in Chrome headless):

```
// minimal fetch-SSE client
const res = await fetch('/v1/streams?model=xyz', { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } });
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while (true) {
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
  // split by newline frames
  const parts = buf.split('\\n');
  buf = parts.pop();
  for (const p of parts) {
    // some frames are truncated here in their runs
    try { console.log('frame', JSON.parse(p)); } catch(e) { console.warn('truncated frame', p.slice(0,80)); }
  }
}
```

jake: can repro locally with headless Chrome and the polyfill. I see the exact pattern: a long quiet period with TCP connection alive (no bytes) then the server FINs. server logs show no error, just worker GC/flush around same time. Not seeing 401s either.

maria: could this be proxy buffering (nginx or CDN) combining chunks and delaying flush until buffer full? That would explain silence followed by larger chunk and partial JSON artifacts if the client splits arbitrarily.

alex: checked edge logs for the customer's region — we have a deploy ~12h ago that changed the streaming flush cadence (deploy-bot: "streaming: flush-throttle adjusted from 8ms to 50ms"). Not huge but could interact with browser timers.

deploy-bot: recent deploy 2026-03-05T02:12:11Z affected runtime flush throttle (PR #4521).

priya: hmm if flush throttle increases, small incremental frames might be coalesced, then if token rotation or middleware touches headers mid-stream we might trigger a connection reset. any evidence of header refresh mid-stream?

jake: instrumented client and saw Authorization header stays constant. However the SDK does run a keepalive/heartbeat in some flows; in the fetch polyfill path we don't currently send a keepalive comment so long silences are possible.

sam: hypothesis: browser TCP keepalives + proxy buffer + longer server-side flush throttle -> apparent silence. If connection closes while client has a partial decoded JSON (split across chunks), client logs truncated frames. Then reconnect resumes and server resends remainder, producing duplicate data.

emily: customer also reported behavior only in default config behind their corporate proxy, not in small test app directly to our edge. so proxy buffer theory fits.

maria: quick mitigation: SDK can emit a lightweight comment frame (eg `:\n`) every 10-15s to keep proxy/connection alive. That avoids coalescing for small server frames. Could we patch canary?

sam: agree. also we should add better client-side join logic: hold incomplete JSON fragments across reconnects for X ms before discarding, to avoid false truncation logs.

jake: PR sketched: add an optional `keepaliveIntervalMs` to browser polyfill (default 15000). On interval, write `:\n` to the underlying stream pump. Also preserve last partial buffer across reconnect attempts up to 2s.

priya: can you add a small repro harness to e2e tests that simulates proxy buffering (coalesce small writes into a delayed flush) so we don't regress?

sam: opening internal ticket RN-7896 and will attach job to backport to js@1.4.x and main. Canaries first.

emily: logged customer case with RN-7896 and told them we're testing a fix. They can enable workaround on their side (disable proxy response buffering) but they'd prefer an SDK fix.

alex: ops will monitor edges for any increased partial-frame errors after revert/patch. For this thread: next steps = canary with keepalive comment + test sim, keep customer updated.

maria: small suggestion to other SDKs (py/go) — they should mirror behavior to avoid cross-language divergence.

sam: timeline: canary in ~6h, if green roll to stable in 24-48h. will post canary artifacts and repro results here.

emily: thanks all, summarizing for customer: root cause likely interaction between our flush-throttle and their proxy buffering causing prolonged silence; mitigation = SDK keepalive + partial-buffer preservation; fix in flight (RN-7896). I'll update the case once canary lands. :thumbsup:
